The validation check `objectName.includes('..')` on line 179 will not catch all path traversal attempts. URL-encoded sequences like `%2e%2e` or double-encoded sequences could bypass this check. Consider using a whitelist approach (e.g., allowing only alphanumeric characters, underscores, and hyphens) or a more robust validation pattern instead of blacklisting specific strings.
```suggestion

    // Normalize and strictly validate object name to prevent path traversal and encoded bypasses
    let normalizedName: string;
    try {
      normalizedName = decodeURIComponent(objectName);
    } catch {
      normalizedName = objectName;
    }

    // Allow only alphanumeric characters, underscores, and hyphens
    const objectNamePattern = /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+$/;
    if (!objectNamePattern.test(normalizedName)) {
      throw new Error(
        'Invalid object name: must contain only alphanumeric characters, underscores, or hyphens'
      );
    }
```
